<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Carries an existing reader's data into the sandbox container the first time
  a sandboxed build launches. Copied into BibleText.app/Contents/Resources by
  the packaging scripts. macOS reads it ONLY on the launch that CREATES the
  container.

  SCHEMA. The operation is the top-level key and holds an array of paths;
  a plain string means "same relative location inside the container", and a
  two-element array means [source, destination]. Paths use the ${Library} and
  ${ApplicationSupport} variables — NOT ${HOME}, which under the sandbox
  already points at the container. This is the format Safari and OneDrive
  ship on this very machine; a manifest in any other shape is ignored in
  silence, which is indistinguishable from a reader having no data.

  WHY IT MATTERS. A sandboxed build is a different app to macOS: its own
  container, and HOME redirected into it. Without this file the app writes a
  new empty preferences file and finds no notes, no reading position, no
  settings and no saved API keys. Nothing is destroyed — the old file is
  still on disk, on the other side of the container boundary — but the app
  cannot tell: it reads an empty store and takes the branch for a genuinely
  new reader, and the deliberate-wipe sentinel cannot fire because it lived
  in the file now out of reach.

  Notes are the part that cannot be replaced, including notes other people
  shared, which exist nowhere else.

  THE PATH IS KEYED "bibletext", NOT THE BUNDLE ID. app.go calls
  NewWithID("bibletext"), and Fyne's identifier wins over the FyneApp.toml
  metadata id when it builds Library/Preferences/fyne/<id>. A manifest
  written against uk.co.bibletext.desktop would migrate nothing, silently,
  and there is no second chance. scripts/check-mac-store-config.py reads the
  identifier out of app.go and fails when this disagrees.

  MOVE, NOT COPY — and this was measured, not chosen. A Copy entry in this
  same manifest is ignored: a sandboxed build launched with one created its
  container and wrote a fresh empty preferences file while the source sat
  untouched beside it. Move carried all of it across. Neither Safari's nor
  OneDrive's manifest uses Copy either.

  The cost is that Move takes the data rather than duplicating it, so a
  direct-download build still installed alongside opens as a new reader
  afterwards. That is the honest trade: Copy would have left both working,
  and Copy does not work.

  Caches are deliberately not migrated: they re-download with progress
  already shown, and the embedded seed keeps the reader in scripture
  meanwhile.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Move</key>
    <array>
        <string>${Library}/Preferences/fyne/bibletext</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
